{"name":"Animal Cracker","hex":"#f4e6ce","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"animal cracker","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, crispy, sweet, baked cake (biscuit or cookie) in the shape of an animal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/animal_cracker"}],"entries":[{"title":"Animal cracker","blocks":[{"text":"An animal cracker is a particular type of cracker or biscuit – cookie in American English – baked in the shape of an animal, usually an animal either at a zoo or a circus, such as a lion, tiger, bear or elephant. The most common variety is light-colored and slightly sweet, but darker chocolate-flavored and colorful frosted varieties are also sold. Although animal crackers tend to be sweet in flavor like cookies, they are made with a layered dough, like crackers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Barnum%27s_animals_examples.JPG/330px-Barnum%27s_animals_examples.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_cracker","wikidata":"Q4764932"},{"title":"Cracker (term)","blocks":[{"text":"Cracker, sometimes cracka or white cracker, is a racial slur directed at white people, used especially with regard to poor rural whites in the Southern United States. It is commonly a pejorative, though is also used in a neutral context, particularly in reference to a native of Florida or Georgia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Georgia_crackers_1873.jpg/330px-Georgia_crackers_1873.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cracker_(term)","wikidata":"Q2567826"},{"title":"Cracker (food)","blocks":[{"text":"A cracker is a flat, dry baked biscuit typically made with flour. Flavorings or seasonings, such as salt, herbs, seeds, or cheese, may be added to the dough or sprinkled on top before or after baking. Crackers are often branded as a nutritious and convenient way to consume a staple food or cereal grain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Crackers_with_herring_and_garlic_sauce.jpg/330px-Crackers_with_herring_and_garlic_sauce.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cracker_(food)","wikidata":"Q856330"},{"title":"Animal","blocks":[{"text":"Animals are multicellular, eukaryotic organisms belonging to the biological kingdom Animalia. Animals consume organic material, breathe oxygen, have muscle cells, can reproduce sexually, and grow from a hollow sphere of cells, the blastula, during embryonic development. With few exceptions, animals are able to move. Animals form a clade, meaning that they arose from a single common ancestor. Over 1.5 million living animal species have been described, of which around 1.05 million are insects, over 85,000 are molluscs, and around 65,000 are vertebrates. It has been estimated there are as many as 7.77 million animal species on Earth. Animal body lengths range from 8.5 μm (0.00033 in) to 33.6 m (110 ft). They have complex ecologies and interactions with each other and their environments, forming intricate food webs. The scientific study of animals is known as zoology, and the study of animal behaviour is known as ethology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Animal_diversity_b.png/330px-Animal_diversity_b.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal","wikidata":"Q729"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Stauffer%27s_animal_crackers.JPG/500px-Stauffer%27s_animal_crackers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Stauffer%27s_animal_crackers.JPG/500px-Stauffer%27s_animal_crackers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stauffer's Animals Crackers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStauffer's_animal_crackers.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Remington_A_cracker_cowboy.jpg/500px-Remington_A_cracker_cowboy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Remington_A_cracker_cowboy.jpg/500px-Remington_A_cracker_cowboy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A \"cracker cowboy\" with his Florida Cracker Horse and dog by Frederic Remington, 1895","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARemington_A_cracker_cowboy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9a/Georgia_cracker_peaches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9a/Georgia_cracker_peaches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Georgia Cracker label depicting a boy with peaches","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeorgia_cracker_peaches.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Army_and_Navy_hard_tack.jpg/500px-Army_and_Navy_hard_tack.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Army_and_Navy_hard_tack.jpg/500px-Army_and_Navy_hard_tack.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reproduction of 19th-century hardtack, in the Army (square) and Navy (round) styles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArmy_and_Navy_hard_tack.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Arare.jpg/250px-Arare.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/